.hcta__foot a:hover { color: #fff; text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 3px; }








/* Filtered-out frames must be removed from the layout AND the a11y tree,
   otherwise a keyboard user can still tab into a photo they can't see. */
.cell.is-hidden { display: none; }

/* ------------------------------------------- portfolio header, compact
 *
 * Eyebrow, title and filter now stack tightly instead of floating apart: the
 * old header (eyebrow, 64px title, a lede, then the filter) spent 430px before
 * the first photograph. The lede ("Portraits and places, shot around Gauteng.")
 * is hidden, not deleted, because the filter now says "Places" and the eyebrow
 * already says "Gauteng" — it was repeating both. It is still in the markup;
 * ask Alwin before removing it.
 *
 * `.page` prefixes are deliberate: `.page h1` (0,1,1) sets margin/line-height
 * and would beat a bare `.phead__h` (0,1,0). Same trap as the home heading,
 * see HOME-LOCKED.md. */
.portfolio .phead {
  max-width: none;
  padding: 28px 0 0;
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 12px;
}
.portfolio .phead .eyebrow { margin: 0; }
.page.portfolio .phead__h {
  font-size: clamp(40px, 5.2vw, 72px);
  line-height: 0.9;
  margin: 0;
}
.portfolio .phead__p { display: none; }



@media (max-width: 760px) {



  .page.portfolio .phead__h { font-size: 44px; }
}

/* ------------------------------------------------ portfolio wall: columns
 *
 * Alwin, 2026-10-01: "maybe we should do a 3 column one for desktop and 2
 * column for mobile only portfolio page, work now it should be as clean and
 * well put together as the home page".
 *
 * This was justified rows (src/rows.ts), which packed FIVE OR SIX frames side by
 * side at 1440. Each row was internally flush and nothing was cropped, but a
 * contact sheet is not a portfolio, and that was the complaint.
 *
 * Now: three columns on a desktop, two on a phone, each frame keeping its own
 * aspect ratio — so nothing is cropped — with the columns packed to an even
 * bottom by `packByHeight()`, the same packer the locked home strip uses. The
 * emitted `--wall-cols` and the grid tracks come from one call to
 * `wallCols()` in pages.ts, so they cannot disagree.
 *
 * `.wall__col` is a real flex column, NOT `display:contents`. That was the bug
 * in an earlier iteration: `contents` dissolved the column boxes and the grid
 * turned row-major, so every track became as tall as its tallest frame and each
 * short frame left a white notch underneath.
 */







/* ------------------------------------------------ portfolio wall behaviour
 *
 * Alwin, 2026-10-01: "when i hover it destroys the crop, lets not make the image
 * bigger or blow up on hover, make it like the home page images".
 *
 * There is no hover preview on the wall, deliberately. The overlay it replaces
 * was measured at 247px of thumbnail swapping to 492px of the same photograph —
 * a clean 2x — with the thumbnail underneath at opacity 0. In a justified row
 * that is a substitution, not a preview: the cell takes a differently-cropped
 * copy whose height and aspect ratio disagree with the frames either side of
 * it. No amount of toning the scale down fixes that, because the row was built
 * around the crop it was given.
 *
 * So the wall uses the home page's treatment, which needs no element of its
 * own — `.shell.is-bw .cell img` in styles.css already renders every frame
 * monochrome at rest and returns it to full colour on hover. Nothing about the
 * layout moves, because nothing about the layout changes.
 *
 * Removing the markup also halves the images on this page: each cell used to
 * carry a thumbnail AND a separate 1200w preview, so 50 frames meant 100 <img>.
 *
 * Tap-to-open lightbox is untouched; that is the real way to see a frame large.
 */
.pf-cell {
  position: relative;
  display: block;
  width: 100%;
  padding: 0;
  border: 0;
  background: none;
  cursor: zoom-in;
  overflow: visible;
}
.pf-cell > .cell { display: block; }

/* THE FILTER BUG. The filter handler sets the `hidden` attribute on the cells it
   wants gone, but `.pf-cell { display: block }` above out-ranks the browser's
   built-in `[hidden] { display: none }`, so every "hidden" frame stayed on screen
   and clicking Landscapes did nothing visible (46 of 50 cells had `hidden` set and
   all 46 were still displayed). Re-assert it with higher specificity. Any element
   that is given `display` AND toggled with `hidden` needs this. */
.pf-cell[hidden] { display: none; }


@media (max-width: 760px) {
  .pf-cell { cursor: pointer; }
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------- about page */

.about { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 0.82fr); gap: 64px; align-items: start; }
.about__body { padding-top: 40px; }
/* The `.page` prefix is required, not decoration.
 *
 * `.page h1` (0,1,1) sets `margin: 0`, and that beats a bare `.about__h`
 * (0,1,0) — the same trap as the home heading documented in HOME-LOCKED.md and
 * in the `.portfolio .phead` note below. Without the prefix, the computed
 * margin-bottom was 0px at BOTH 390 and 1440, so the 58px heading sat flush
 * against the first line of copy with zero gap:
 *     gapH1ToFirstP: 0    (measured, 1440 and 390)
 * The rule above this comment already declared `margin-bottom: 32px`; it was
 * simply never winning. */
.page .about__h { font-size: clamp(34px, 5vw, 58px); margin-bottom: 32px; }
.about__prose { max-width: 46ch; }
.about__cta { margin-top: 34px; }
